{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"tid-1","run_id":"run-1","tool_use_id":"toolu-1","status":"completed","output_file":"/tmp/claude-tmp/-tmp-work-r7-budget-during-call-2/sess-1/tasks/tid-1.output","summary":"The Last Light at Harrow Point\n\nFor thirty-one years, Elias Thorne climbed the spiral stairs of the Harrow Point lighthouse every dusk, and every dusk he lit the lamp. The task never changed. He trimmed the wick, checked the lens for salt haze, wound the clockwork that turned the great glass prisms, and watched the beam swing out across the black water like a slow, patient arm reaching for ships that might be lost.\n\nThe island had nine residents when he arrived and two when he left. The fishing boats had gone to the harbor town years before, and the ferry came only when the weather allowed. Elias had come to Harrow Point to be alone. He told himself that was the whole reason, and for a long time it was true.\n\nHis wife, Marta, had died in the spring of his second year on the rock. He buried her on the eastern slope, where the wind bent the grass flat, and he went back up the stairs that night because the light did not know he was grieving. Ships did not wait for grief. That was the first lesson the sea taught him, and the lighthouse reinforced it every evening for decades.\n\nOver the years he kept a logbook. Weather, visibility, the names of vessels he could identify through the telescope. Most entries were brief. *Fog thick until midnight. Freighter Cassia passed northward, lights steady.* He never wrote about himself. The logbook was for the ships, not for him.\n\nIn the autumn of his thirtieth year, a storm came in from the southwest with no warning the radio could give. The waves broke over the lower rocks and the lamp room shook under his feet. Through the rain he saw a small fishing boat, its running lights flickering, drifting toward the reef. He kept the beam steady, sweeping it across the water again and again, and he sounded the horn every few minutes until his throat ached. The boat turned. It slipped past the reef and into the cove, and by morning a young man named Tobias Reyes was sitting in the keeper's kitchen, wrapped in a wool blanket, drinking tea with shaking hands.\n\nTobias stayed three days until the sea calmed. He asked questions. Why do you stay out here? Doesn't it get lonely? Elias gave the answers he had always given, practical and brief. But on the third evening, as they climbed the stairs together to light the lamp, Tobias stopped halfway and looked out through the narrow window at the darkening sea.\n\n\"You know they can see this from forty miles out,\" he said. \"Every night. Someone out there is steering by it right now, and they don't even know your name.\"\n\nElias had never thought of it that way. He had thought of the light as a duty, something he owed to the water. Now he saw it differently, as a kind of conversation carried on across distances, with strangers he would never meet.\n\nWhen Tobias left, he did not say goodbye so much as promise to come back, and he did, every spring for the next several years, with his own children in tow.\n\nElias kept the light until his hands grew too unsteady for the clockwork. The mainland automated the station the winter he turned seventy-two. On his last evening he climbed the stairs one final time, trimmed the wick, and lit the lamp. The beam swept out across the water, steady and unhurried.\n\nHe wrote in the logbook one last time: *Clear skies. Light lit. Visibility excellent.* Then he closed the book, set it on the sill, and went down the stairs without looking back, trusting that somewhere out in the dark, someone would still be steering by the light.","usage":{"total_tokens":6293,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":8040},"uuid":"uuid-59","session_id":"sess-1"}
